\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \(P(n)\) treated explicitly as a function of a real variable \(x\), by comparison against the genuine continuous function \(x\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow e^{-Cx}\) (left, linear scale, \(x\le 45\)) and \(x\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow -Cx\) (right, logarithmic scale, \(x\le 150\)). The discrete values \(P(n)\) for prime \(n\) lie inside the continuous envelope \(\pm e^{-Cx}\); composite \(n\) sit on the axis. Both panels display the same first-order law of Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:scale} as an explicit function of \(x\), not merely as a discrete sequence.}}{4}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:Px-envelope}{{2}{4}{\(P(n)\) treated explicitly as a function of a real variable \(x\), by comparison against the genuine continuous function \(x\mapsto e^{-Cx}\) (left, linear scale, \(x\le 45\)) and \(x\mapsto -Cx\) (right, logarithmic scale, \(x\le 150\)). The discrete values \(P(n)\) for prime \(n\) lie inside the continuous envelope \(\pm e^{-Cx}\); composite \(n\) sit on the axis. Both panels display the same first-order law of Theorem~\ref {thm:scale} as an explicit function of \(x\), not merely as a discrete sequence}{figure.caption.3}{}}
